	tags: for, digits, zeros,
}
-->

<statement>
<p>
	Since 5 steps of this process were needed, we say that has a kaprekar number of .
</p>

<tabular valign="top" top="minor">
	<col width="12%"/>
	<col width="88%"/>
	<row bottom="minor">
		<cell>Inputs:</cell>
		<cell>
			<p><pf>number</pf> (1 x 1) integer — positive integer.</p>
		</cell>
	</row>
	<row bottom="minor">
		<cell>Outputs:</cell>
		<cell>
			<p><pf>number</pf> (1 x 1) number — Kap_number (1 x 1) The Kaprekar number of <pf>number</pf>.</p>
		</cell>
	</row>
	<row>
		<cell>Details:</cell>
		<cell><p>Numbers such as 2222 will yield a difference of zero and will never result in 6174. In this case, we say the number of Kaprekar number is infinity since we will never get there. Your program should output</p></cell>
	</row>
	<row>
		<cell/>
		<cell><p>inf</p></cell>
	</row>
	<row>
		<cell/>
		<cell><p>, MATLAB's notation for infinity.</p></cell>
	</row>
	<row>
		<cell/>
		<cell><p>For numbers less than 1000, the descending digits must have trailing zeros. For example, if n = 7 (i.e., 0007), the subtraction step is 7</p></cell>
	</row>
	<row>
		<cell/>
		<cell><p>000</p></cell>
	</row>
	<row>
		<cell/>
		<cell><p>− 7 = 6993.</p></cell>
	</row>
	<row bottom="minor">
		<cell/>
		<cell><p>This program should use multiple programs from the previous homework assignment. Paste a working copy of each program you call in this function at the bottom of the file.</p></cell>
	</row>
</tabular>

<p>
	Examples:
<program>
	<code>
		kaprekar_number(1111)
		inf
		kaprekar_number(5265)
		4
	</code>
</program>
</p>
</statement>
